 { Black is slightly better due to his sounder pawn structure - one pawn stops two - but White should be able to hold without much effort. } 1. { -KEY- } Re3 $2 { This is not a losing move, but a step in the wrong direction, since any structural deficiencies will be more strongly felt in a pawn ending. } ( { The active } 1. Rc8 { was just one alternative, staying in the safer waters of a rook ending. } ) 
Re5 $1 2. Rxe5 $6 { There was no reason to help Black in activating his king. } ( { Correct was } 2. Kf3 Rxe3 3. Kxe3 Ke5 4. Kf3 Kd4 5. h4 $1 { securing a draw after } gxh4 6. g5 Ke5 7. Kg4 ) 
Kxe5 3. Ke3 $2 { Despite its natural appearance, this move loses as the king is displaced in relation to the plan with h2-h4 that we just saw. } ( { Thus } 3. Kf3 $1 Kd4 4. h4 $1 { was called for, transposing to the previous line. Note that the importance of precise calculation grows once the pawn ending is reached. } ) 
Kd5 4. h4 ( { Or } 4. Kf3 e5 $1 5. Kg3 e4 6. h4 gxh4 7. Kxh4 e3 8. Kg3 Kd4 9. Kg2 Kd3 $1 { and wins. } ) 
gxh4 5. g5 ( 5. Kf4 { doesn't help either in view of } e5 ) 
Ke5 6. g6 Kf6 7. Kf4 Kxg6 
