 { The previous examples showed that a transposition to a rook ending tends to make the defender's task a bit easier. From this follows that the stronger side often does right in avoiding that type of ending. Here is one illustrative example. Black is about to win a pawn, but first he should have reflected about which type of ending suits him best. } 1... { -KEY- } e5 $1 ( { The game instead went } 1... Rxb6 $2 { Now White manages to reach a rook ending, in which an extra pawn on one single flank often doesn't suffice for victory; besides, here the defender has already achieved the ideal f2-g3-h4 structure. } 2. Rxd4 Rxc6 3. Ra4 $1 { Preparing a rear attack. } e5 4. Ra7 Kf6 5. Rh7 Kg6 6. Re7 f4 $6 { Black loses his patience. } ( 6... Rc5 { was better, preparing the same advance, although after } 7. Kg2 f4 8. gxf4 exf4 9. Re6 Kf5 10. Re8 Rc2 11. Rf8 Ke4 12. Re8 Kd3 13. Rf8 { White holds. } ) 
7. Rxe5 f3 8. Kg1 $1 Rc1 { Draw agreed, in view of } Rc1 9. Kh2 Rf1 10. Rg5 Kh6 11. Rg6 $1 { etc - Ivkov. } ) 
 ( 1... Rb1 $6 { is also a bit rushed, since after } 2. Kg2 e5 3. b7 Kd6 { , compared to the main line, White could now steal a tempo by the deflective } 4. Rc1 $1 { so that after } Rb6 5. Be8 Rxb7 6. Bxh5 { , Black won't have ...Rb1 as in the 4.Bxh5 line below. } ) 
2. b7 Kd6 { Was called for - Ivkov; e.g. } 3. Be8 ( { Both } 3. Bg2 { and } 3. Bh1 { fail to } Rb1 ) 
Rxb7 4. Rc1 ( { By the way, } 4. Bxh5 $2 { loses outright to } Rb1 5. Kg2 Ra1 $1 { when zugzwang rules out the idea of a sacrifice on g4, and } 6. Rb4 Kc5 7. Rb8 Nf3 { leads to a quick mate; } ) 
Rh7 { With excellent winning chances in the presence of the minor pieces. } 
