 { Of course a transposition to a rook ending isn't the best choice for the defender on every occasion, and conversely, sometimes it can represent a good option for the stronger side. Here are two related examples.White is under pressure, and must now make a choice regarding his knight. } 1. { -KEY- } Ne2+ $2 { In the ensuing rook ending, the defender's king won't be able to return to its correct post in front of the pawn. } ( { Thus } 1. Nh5 { was called for; e.g. } Kg5 2. Rc5 Kh4 3. Kf2 { and White defends - Baburin, who also mentions that the best Black can hope for here is a rook and bishop vs. rook ending. That is a theoretical draw (albeit not without difficulty in practice) except in some specific positions, one of which we saw in the } gxf5 { subline of Example 380 (Carlsen-Caruana). } ) 
Bxe2+ 2. Kxe2 Kg3 $1 { The rest is easy. } 3. Kf1 Ra1+ 4. Ke2 Kg2 5. Rc2 Rf1 6. Ke3+ Kg1 7. Rc8 g3 8. Ke2 ( { After } 8. Ke2 { Black played } Rf7 { however, much simpler was } ( 8. Ke2 g2 9. Rh8 Rf7 10. Ke1 Re7 11. Kd2 Re5 { followed by a bridge } ) 
9. Rc1 Kg2 10. Ra1 Re7 { and went on to win. } ) 

