 { Given the high drawing tendency in endings with opposite-coloured bishops, a transposition to such a type of ending can sometimes be used as a defensive weapon.Black is under pressure, but Salov came up with a simple solution. } 1... { -KEY- } c5 $1 { Sacrificing a pawn in order to reach an ending with opposite-coloured bishops. } ( { Other moves, such as } 1... g6 { might run into } 2. Qc5 $5 Qxc5 3. Bxc5 Rb7 4. a6 $1 { with a clear advantage for White, who could prepare an invasion with the rooks down the b-file. } ) 
2. Rxc5 Rxc5 3. Rxc5 Rxc5 4. Qxc5 Qxc5 5. Bxc5 a6 { Now it is difficult to see how White can capitalize on his extra pawn. } 6. Bb6 Bd7 7. Bd4 g6 8. Kh4 Be6 9. Kg5 Bd7 10. Kh6 ( { If } 10. g4 { then simply } Be6 ) 
Be6 11. Bc5 Bd7 { The game later ended in a draw. } 
